course: dictionary
lesson: 6657
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. «Nut» is a good example. The meaning you need depends on the context, which means the words and sentences around it. Read the clues first, then choose the meaning that fits.
explain: «Grey squirrels bury nuts in many separate spots» tells you a nut is a fruit with a hard shell and a seed inside. You can check this by swapping in your meaning: a squirrel buries a fruit with a hard shell, and that makes sense.
explain: Other clues point to other meanings. Nuts and bolts hold things together, so the metal meaning fits when the text is about building or fixing things. A football nut is a person who is very keen on football, so the informal meaning fits when someone is going wild about a sport.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Nut» means a fruit with a hard shell and a seed inside, such as a walnut or hazelnut.
fact: «Nut» can also mean a small piece of metal with a hole in it that screws onto a bolt.
fact: Informal «nut» means a person who is very keen on something, as in «a football nut».
fact: «Nut-free» means containing no nuts, which keeps people with a nut allergy safe.
exercise: «Grey squirrels bury nuts in many separate spots.» What is a nut in this sentence? | A hard problem to solve | A person who is very keen on something | *A fruit with a hard shell and a seed inside | A small piece of metal with a hole in it
exercise: Sam says, «He's a real football nut.» What does nut mean here? | A small piece of metal with a hole in it | *A person who is very keen on football | A fruit with a hard shell | A seed that a squirrel buries
exercise: «Nuts and bolts» hold things together. In this phrase, what is a nut? | A squirrel's hiding place | A silly person acting crazy | *A small piece of metal with a hole that screws onto a bolt | A fruit with a hard shell
test: «The cashew nut grows under a fleshy cashew apple.» Which meaning of nut is used? | A problem that is hard to crack | *A fruit with a hard shell and a seed inside | A person who is very keen on something | A small piece of metal with a hole in it
test: Ella's school is «nut-free» because some pupils have a nut allergy. What does nut-free mean? | *Containing no nuts, so it is safe for people with a nut allergy | A school with a hard problem to solve | Full of nuts for everyone to share | A school where people are very keen on nuts
exam: Ben reads, «Our school is nut-free because of allergies.» Which meaning of nut does this use? | A small piece of metal with a hole in it | *A fruit with a hard shell and a seed inside | A person who is very keen on something | A hard problem to crack
